      "hasOCR": false,
      "language": "中英",
      "abstract": "In view of the existing blind image quality assessment only focusing on the local or global features of the image，it is difficult to effectively aggregate features，an evaluation method based on sparse representation of multi-level information was proposed．First，the synthetic distortion technique was used to construct a large-scale dataset．And then the image patches were extracted from the original image as the primary visual information to establish the local descriptor set．Meanwhile，high-level semantic information was extracted by convolutional neural network to establish the global descriptor set．On these bases，sparse coding was used as unsupervised learning to train the local and global descriptor codebooks．Finally，the sparse coding coefficients of the targeted training samples on these two types of codebooks were aggregated as image representation，and the existing labels were combined to train a support vector regression (SVR) model to predict the image quality．The experimental results on the single database like LIVE，CSIQ and TID2013 show that the proposed algorithm has strong subjective and objective consistency and the overall performance is better than other advanced methods．The results demonstrate that the proposed algorithm has strong generalization ability and offensive and defensive competitiveness，which is beneficial to the wide applications of image quality",
